---
import recording from "./crash-replay.json";

const sides = ["usual", "pybeam"] as const;
const format = (value: number | string, unit = "") =>
  typeof value === "number" ? value.toFixed(unit === " ms" ? 2 : 0) + unit : value;
const rows = [
  { label: "Reply p99", values: recording.baselineP99, unit: " ms" },
];
const faultRows = [
  { label: `Users cut off (of ${recording.otherRoomSockets})`, values: recording.disconnected, unit: "" },
  { label: "Sends with no reply", values: recording.unacknowledged, unit: "" },
  { label: "Reply p99", values: recording.faultP99, unit: " ms" },
];
---
<div data-crash-replay>
  <p class="replay-context">Recorded measurements · {recording.trials} trials</p>
  <p>
    Step through the published native-crash results. This is a static walkthrough,
    not a live test or a recording of individual messages.
    Values are medians with minimum–maximum ranges across three trials.
  </p>

  <table class="replay-boundaries">
    <caption>Each outline is a process boundary</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Usual stack</th><th scope="col">PyBEAM</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr>
      <td>
        <div class="process-boundary">
          <strong>Web worker</strong>
          <span>Chat connections</span>
          <span>Crashing request</span>
        </div>
        <p>One of eight workers. Users from different rooms share it.</p>
      </td>
      <td>
        <div class="process-boundary">
          <strong>Chat server</strong>
          <span>Chat connections</span>
        </div>
        <div class="process-boundary">
          <strong>Separate child process</strong>
          <span>Crashing request</span>
        </div>
      </td>
    </tr></tbody>
  </table>

  <div data-replay-stage="0">
    <h2 id="before-the-crash" tabindex="-1">Before the crash</h2>
    <p>
      Both stacks receive the same workload: eight rooms with four connected users
      each, sending four messages per second per user. No background jobs run.
      We watch the 28 users in rooms other than the fault target.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Other-room replies during the 3-second baseline</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Metric</th><th scope="col">Usual stack</th><th scope="col">PyBEAM</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map(({ label, values, unit }) => <tr>
        <th scope="row">{label}</th>
        {sides.map((side) => <td>
          <strong>{format(values[side].median, unit)}</strong>
          <span class="replay-range">{format(values[side].min, unit)}–{format(values[side].max, unit)}</span>
        </td>)}
      </tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div data-replay-stage="1">
    <h2 id="during-the-crash" tabindex="-1">A request crashes its process</h2>
    <p>
      The request calls <code>os.abort()</code>, like a native extension crashing.
      In the usual stack, the web worker dies with its connections. In PyBEAM,
      the separate task process dies; the chat server stays running.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Other-room impact during the 3-second fault window</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Metric</th><th scope="col">Usual stack</th><th scope="col">PyBEAM</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{faultRows.map(({ label, values, unit }) => <tr>
        <th scope="row">{label}</th>
        {sides.map((side) => <td>
          <strong>{format(values[side].median, unit)}</strong>
          <span class="replay-range">{format(values[side].min, unit)}–{format(values[side].max, unit)}</span>
        </td>)}
      </tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <strong>Unbounded</strong> means some scheduled sends still had no reply
      after an 8-second drain, including sends missed during reconnection.
      It does not mean the server stayed down forever.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div data-replay-stage="2">
    <h2 id="after-the-crash" tabindex="-1">A healthy server does not erase an interruption</h2>
    <p>
      Every post-fault health check returned HTTP 200 on both stacks. Other-room
      HTTP probes had no failures, even though some chat connections disconnected
      in the usual stack.
    </p>
    <p>
      A replacement web worker can serve new connections; it cannot preserve the
      dead worker’s existing connections. Running dangerous work in a separate
      process kept that crash away from PyBEAM’s chat connections in these trials.
    </p>
    <p>
      This compares failure boundaries, not maximum throughput. Zero observed
      disconnects is not a guarantee of zero latency impact or durable messages.
      Neither example retries lost jobs or persists room counters.
    </p>
    <p>
      A conventional Python application can isolate dangerous work in a separate
      process too. The difference here is where these two examples run that work,
      not immunity to crashes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="replay-controls" data-replay-controls hidden>
    <button type="button" data-replay-next>Show the crash</button>
    <span data-replay-position>Before the crash</span>
  </div>

  <details class="replay-method">
    <summary>How to read these measurements</summary>
    <p>
      Bold values are medians; the line below is the minimum–maximum across three
      trials, not one run. Reply p99 is the 99th percentile of acknowledgement
      latency. The fault window starts when the client sends the crash request;
      late replies keep their full latency.
    </p>
    <p>
      Linux x86-64, eight CPUs shared with the loopback client. Stacks were loaded
      sequentially, with the dashboard stopped: PyBEAM on Python 3.14.10t; the
      usual stack on Python 3.14.10 with eight web workers and a shared key/value
      cache. Worker assignment and the machine affect the results.
    </p>
    <p><a href={recording.source}>Published measurements and reproduction instructions</a></p>
  </details>
</div>

<script>
  import "./crash-replay.js";
</script>

<style>
  .replay-context { font-weight: 650; margin-block-start: 1.5rem; }
  table { inline-size: 100%; display: table; table-layout: auto; overflow-wrap: normal; }
  caption { text-align: start; font-weight: 650; padding-block: .75rem; }
  th, td { vertical-align: top; padding: .65rem .5rem; }
  .replay-boundaries th { inline-size: 50%; }
  .process-boundary { display: grid; gap: .35rem; border: 1px solid var(--graphical-fg); padding: .75rem; }
  .process-boundary + .process-boundary { margin-block-start: .75rem; }
  .replay-range { display: block; color: var(--muted-fg); }
  .replay-controls { display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; margin-block: 1.5rem; }
  [hidden] { display: none !important; }
  button { padding: .6rem 1rem; min-block-size: 2.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--fg); border-radius: 0; color: var(--bg); background: var(--fg); cursor: pointer; }
  button:hover { color: var(--fg); background: var(--box-bg); }
  button:focus-visible, summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 4px; }
  h2:focus { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 4px; }
  [data-replay-position] { color: var(--muted-fg); }
  .replay-method { margin-block-start: 1.5rem; border-block-start: 1px solid var(--graphical-fg); padding-block-start: 1rem; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; }
  @media (max-width: 28rem) {
    th, td { padding-inline: .25rem; }
    .replay-boundaries th { inline-size: 50%; }
  }
</style>
